    <h1>Roth vs. Pre-Tax 401(k) in 2027</h1>
    <p class="subtitle">
      <strong>Short answer:</strong> Pre-tax 401(k) contributions reduce your 2027 taxable income at your current marginal bracket (10%–37%), while Roth 401(k) contributions are taxed now and grow tax-free. If your 2026 FICA wages exceeded <strong>$155,000</strong>, any 2027 catch-up contributions above $25,500 must be Roth.
    </p>

    <div class="cta-back">
      <a class="btn btn-primary" href="/" style="text-decoration: none;">Model Your Roth / Pre-Tax Split →</a>
    </div>

    <h2>When Pre-Tax Usually Wins</h2>
    <ul>
      <li><strong>High current marginal bracket:</strong> If you are in the 24%, 32%, 35%, or 37% federal bracket in 2027 (above $109,125 single or $218,250 married filing jointly per Rev. Proc. 2026-35) and expect a lower effective rate in retirement.</li>
      <li><strong>High-tax states:</strong> Pre-tax deferrals also shelter state income tax today if you plan to retire in a lower-tax state.</li>
    </ul>

    <h2>When Roth Usually Wins</h2>
    <ul>
      <li><strong>Lower early-career brackets:</strong> In the 10% or 12% brackets (up to $52,025 single / $104,050 joint taxable income), paying tax now locks in historically low rates.</li>
      <li><strong>Tax diversification &amp; RMDs:</strong> Roth 401(k) balances are not subject to Required Minimum Distributions during the original owner’s lifetime.</li>
      <li><strong>High-earner catch-up rule:</strong> Under <a href="/roth-catch-up-high-earners/">SECURE 2.0 Section 603 ($155,000 rule)</a>, participants 50+ with 2026 W-2 Box 3 FICA wages over $155,000 must make catch-up contributions as Roth.</li>
    </ul>

    <h2>Can You Split Both in the Same Year?</h2>
    <p>
      Yes. The <strong>$25,500</strong> base limit ($34,000 at 50+, $37,250 at 60–63) is a combined cap across pre-tax and Roth deferrals. See also <a href="/401k-take-home-pay-impact/">how pre-tax vs. Roth affects take-home pay</a>.
    </p>
  </article>

